  sections: [
    { id: "goal", title: "Start with the role you want", paragraphs: ["An effective resume does not try to document your entire professional life. It selects the information that explains why you fit a specific type of role.", "Before writing, review several similar openings and note the responsibilities, tools, and knowledge you genuinely possess. Use that reference to decide what to emphasize without copying the posting or inventing experience."], bullets: ["Define one or two related target roles.", "Identify requirements that appear repeatedly.", "Keep only skills and achievements you can support."] },
    { id: "structure", title: "Use a structure that is easy to scan", paragraphs: ["For most candidates, name and professional title, contact details, summary, experience, skills, and education are enough. Add languages, certifications, or projects when they contribute relevant evidence.", "Order sections around your strongest material: an experienced candidate will usually prioritize work history, while a junior candidate may lead with education and projects."], bullets: ["Name and professional title.", "Necessary contact details.", "Concise professional summary.", "Relevant experience or projects.", "Skills related to the role.", "Applicable education, languages, and certifications."] },
    { id: "summary", title: "Write a summary that adds context", paragraphs: ["In three or four lines, explain what you do, your most relevant experience, and the value you can contribute. Avoid phrases such as “responsible and passionate professional” when they provide no evidence.", "Example: “Administrative analyst with four years of experience preparing reports, reconciling information, and following up with vendors. Advanced Excel user who reduced closing time by standardizing reporting templates.”"] },
    { id: "experience", title: "Describe contributions, not only duties", paragraphs: ["Each entry should identify the employer, role, location or work arrangement, and dates. Then use short bullets to explain actions and outcomes.", "Instead of “Responsible for inventory,” try “Organized inventory for 1,200 items and reduced count discrepancies through weekly reviews.” Do not add estimated numbers you cannot defend."] },
    { id: "skills", title: "Choose relevant, demonstrable skills", paragraphs: ["Group technical skills that help someone evaluate your profile: tools, methods, languages, or profession-specific knowledge. Soft skills become more credible when your experience also demonstrates them.", "You do not need to fill the section. A short list aligned with the role is more useful than twenty generic terms."] },
    { id: "design", title: "Protect readability and space", paragraphs: ["Use consistent hierarchy, sufficient contrast, and legible text sizes. Avoid ambiguous skill bars, excessive decoration, and columns that compress the content too far.", "One page works well for junior or focused profiles. Two pages can be reasonable when you have enough relevant experience; do not shrink the type until it becomes difficult to read merely to force one page."] },
    { id: "review", title: "Review your resume before sending it", paragraphs: ["Confirm that your title, summary, and first experience entries support the target role. Read every bullet and remove repetition, outdated details with no value, and claims you could not explain in an interview."], bullets: ["Your email address and phone number are correct.", "Dates use a consistent format.", "There are no spelling errors or placeholder text.", "Your main skills are supported by evidence.", "The PDF preserves the layout and has a professional filename."] },
  ],
